X:4
T:Galway Bay
C:Music: Tony Small
C:Lyrics: Francis Fahy (1854-1935)
D:Dolores Keane & John Faulkner - Sail Óg Rua
Q:1/4=160
M:3/4
L:1/4
K:G
%
d | "G" G2 B | d2 g | "C" e>d e | "G" d2 B | "Em" B A B | "C" G2 G | "G" G3- | G2 d |
w: It's far a-way I am_ to-day from scenes_ I roamed a boy._ And
"D" d2 d | a2 f | "G" g2 B | "C" c2 d | e2 g | g f e | "D" d3- | d2 d |
w: long a-go the hour I know I first saw Ill--in-ois._ But
d2 d | a2 f | "G" g2 B | "C" c2 d | e2 g | g f e | "D" d3- | d2 d |
w: time nor tide nor wa-ters wide could wean my heart_ a-way._ For
"G" G2 B | d2 g | "C" e d e | "G" d2 B | "Em" B A B | "C" G2 G | "G" G3- | G2 z |]
w: e-ver true it flies_ to you, my dear_ old Gal-way Bay._
%
W: 1. It's far away I am today from scenes I roamed a boy.
W: And long ago the hour I know I first saw Illinois.
W: But time nor tide nor waters wide could wean my heart away.
W: For ever true it flies to you, my dear old Galway Bay.
W:
W: 2. Oh, grey and bleak, by shore and creek, the rugged rocks abound,
W: But sweet and green the grass between, as grows on Irish ground,
W: So friendship fond, all wealth abound, and love that lives always,
W: Bless each poor home beside your foam, my dear old Galway Bay.
W:
W: 3. Had I youth's blood and hopeful mood and heart of fire once more,
W: For all the gold the world might hold I′d never leave your shore,
W: I'd be content with whatever God sent with neighbours old and gray,
W: And I'd lay my bones, ′neath churchyard stones, beside you, Galway Bay.
W:
W: 4. The blessing of a poor old man be with you night and day,
W: The blessing of a lonely man whose heart will soon be clay;
W: It's all the Heaven I'll ask of God upon my dying day,
W: My soul to soar for ever more above you, Galway Bay.
%
% Source: sources/dmc-lizmilner-com/pages/viewabc.vbhtml_918bc207.abc

X:5
T:Lark in the Morning, The
M:3/4
L:1/4
I:tuplets 2 1 0
K:C
%
%
z2 C/>E/ | "C" G G G/>F/ | E C E | "F" F F F/>E/ | "G" D2 E/>F/ |
w: Lay_ still my fond_ shep-herd and don't you rise_ yet. It's a
%
"C" G/>A/ _B A | G/>E/ C (3D/E/D/ | C/>D/ E "G" D/>C/ | "C" C3 |]
w: fine_ dew-y morn--ing and_ be-sides, my love, it is wet.
%
%%leftmargin 2in
%%rightmargin 2in
%
W: 1. Lay still my fond shepherd and don't you rise yet
W: It's a fine dewy morning and besides, my love, it is wet.
W:
W: 2. Oh let it be wet my love and ever so cold
W: I will rise my fond Floro and away to my fold.
W:
W: 3. Oh no, my bright Floro, it is no such thing
W: It's a bright sun a-shining and the lark is on the wing.
W:
W: 4. Oh the lark in the morning she rises from her nest
W: And she mounts in the air with the dew on her breast
W:
W: 5. And like a pretty ploughboy she'll whistle and sing
W: And at night she will return to her own nest again
W:
W: 6. When the ploughboy has done all he's got for to do
W: He trips down to the meadows where the grass is all cut down.
W:
W: 7. Oh the lark in the morning she rises from her nest
W: And she climbs to the dawn with the dew on her breast
W:
W: 8. And like a pretty ploughboy she'll whistle and sing
W: And at night she will return to her own nest again
%
% Source: sources/dmc-lizmilner-com/pages/viewabc.vbhtml_702046f1.abc

